# Carve in Zensical

Every page here is a `.crv` file. `zensical-carve prepare` rendered it, and all
*32 Carve extensions* are enabled, so this is the widest surface the language
offers rather than the default profile.

::: note "What to look at"
The left navigation is Zensical's. The table of contents on the right is
Zensical's too, built from headings this plugin hands back to it as Markdown.
Everything between them is Carve.
:::

## The inline set

Carve's mnemonic is that the markup /looks/ like its output. Slashes lean, so
they are italic. A star is heavier, so it is bold.

|= Written |= Renders |= Markdown would say |
| `/italic/` | /italic/ | `*italic*` |
| `*bold*` | *bold* | `**bold**` |
| `_underline_` | _underline_ | nothing - needs an extension |
| `~strike~` | ~strike~ | `~~strike~~` |
| `=highlight=` | =highlight= | nothing - needs an extension |
| `` `code` `` | `code` | same |
| `{^sup^}` `{,sub,}` | E = mc{^2^}, H{,2,}O | needs two extensions |
^ The five marks Carve spells with one delimiter each.

Braces are what keep superscript and subscript from firing inside a word, a
URL, or a path. There is no bare `^x^` to go wrong.

## Symbols

`:smile:` renders :smile:, `:rocket:` renders :rocket:, and `:heart:` renders
:heart:. The parsing is core Carve, but the map from a name to a glyph is a
render option rather than an extension - a document that reaches an engine
without one renders `:smile:` as its own source text.

This site sets `emoji = "twemoji"`, so a shortcode here becomes the same
element a Markdown page on this site gets, from Zensical's own emoji index.
A name needs a word boundary in front of it, so `a:b:c` stays a:b:c and a time
like 10:30: is left alone.

## Typography happens on its own

`->` becomes ->, `<-` becomes <-, `<->` becomes <->, and `=>` becomes =>.
Comparisons too: `!=` is !=, `<=` is <=, `>=` is >=. Then `(c)`, `(r)`, `(tm)`
and `+-` are (c), (r), (tm) and +-, an ellipsis is ..., and dashes widen -- like
this -- or --- like this.

## Semantic spans

Three names are core and four more arrive with the `semantic-span` extension:

Press [Ctrl]{kbd}+[C]{kbd} to copy. The [HTML]{abbr="HyperText Markup Language"}
specification defines [x]{var}, and a program prints [done]{samp}.
A [dfn]{dfn="a term being defined"} carries its own definition,
and [Dune]{cite} is a title.

## Links that write themselves

A cross-reference fills in its own text: </#the-inline-set>. An external link is
<https://zensical.org> and gets its own class from the `external-links`
extension. A footnote reads like a link.[^why]

`[[Wikilinks]]` stay literal here on purpose. The extension is enabled, but a
page name only becomes a URL through a generator the host supplies, and this
demo supplies none - which is the taxonomy rule working, not a defect.

[^why]: And resolves here, with a link back up. Footnotes are core in Carve, not an extension.

## Spoilers and swatches

Two small Tier-3 extensions. A spoiler hides :spoiler[the answer] until you
hover it, and a color swatch turns a value into a chip: :color[#2563eb].
